  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cool and dry weather through tonight. \n\nSurface high pressure will dominate across the region through \ntonight. Temperatures will fall into the low to mid 40s across \nwestern NY and the upper 30s to low 40s across north-central NY this \nevening. Overnight low temperatures will fall to the 30s in most \nareas, with some 20s across the North Country and in some of the \ncolder Southern Tier valleys. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Well above normal temperatures Sunday afternoon \nthrough the end of the week.\n\nThe pattern becomes amplified across the CONUS with a deep trough \nacross the Plains and a ridge building across the east next week. \nSurface high pressure will persist across the eastern seaboard and \nmaintain mostly dry weather through Wednesday. Warm air advection \nwill strengthen across the region through mid-week. A few showers \ncan't be ruled out on the leading edge of the warm-air advection \nTuesday. The warmest days are expected to be Tuesday and Wednesday \nwith daytime highs in the mid 60s to low 70s on Tuesday, and the \nupper 60s to mid 70s on Wednesday. This setup translates to an \nunusual event for the region in early November according to the \nECMWF EFI. South winds will increase through mid-week. Gusty winds \nare possible on Wednesday. \n\nA narrow corridor of deep moisture ahead of the upper level trough \naxis will slowly work it's way across the region Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday. A shortwave trough coupled with a cold front will \nsupport unsettled weather across the region Thursday through Friday. \nThere remains high uncertainty in rainfall amounts late in the\nweek. Temperatures will remain above normal through the end of\nthe week.",
